  "avoidBefore": "lower",
  "energy": "Jumps, vaults and climbs are short maximal efforts using the short-burst (ATP-PC) energy system, linked into runs of 20-60 s that also tax the glycolytic system, while long sessions rest on an aerobic base.",
  "summary": "Parkour demands explosive jumping, high landing forces, a strong grip and pull for climb-ups, and ankles and wrists that tolerate repeated impacts. The plan builds leg strength and jump power, landing control on both legs, pulling and grip strength, and ankle and wrist resilience.",
  "evidence": [
    {
      "claim": "In 180 traceurs, ankle injuries (mostly sprains) were the most common at 2.3 per 1,000 h; those who did no physical conditioning had more ankle and knee injuries.",
      "source": "Grospretre & El Khattabi 2022, Movement and Sport Sciences",
      "url": "https://www.mov-sport-sciences.org/articles/sm/full_html/2022/01/sm210045/sm210045.html",
      "grade": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Parkour precision and roll landings cut peak landing force by about 38-43% and loading rate by about 54-63% compared with a traditional drop landing.",
      "source": "Puddle & Maulder 2013, Journal of Sports Science and Medicine",
      "url": "https://www.jssm.org/jssm-12-122.xml-Fulltext",
      "grade": "moderate"
    },
    {
      "claim": "In 161 practitioners, 70% reported an injury in a year; the ankle (28.5%), knee (18.4%) and wrist (12.6%) were most affected, often on precision jumps.",
      "source": "Giner Gran 2020, SPORT TK",
      "url": "https://revistas.um.es/sportk/article/download/412551/278711",
      "grade": "moderate"
    }
  ],
